data: {"type":"tool_execution_start","toolCallId":"toolu_012WhXcnv9YQ1BREbXHqHMEy","toolName":"probe","args":{"question":"Let $h(x) = f\\big(f(x)\\big)$. You're given:\n\n| | value |\n|---|---|\n| $f(1)$ | $2$ |\n| $f(2)$ | $5$ |\n| $f'(1)$ | $3$ |\n| $f'(2)$ | $4$ |\n| $f'(5)$ | $6$ |\n\nWhat is $h'(1)$?","options":[{"label":"$12$","value":"a"},{"label":"$9$","value":"b"},{"label":"$18$","value":"c"},{"label":"$24$","value":"d"}],"explanation":"The outer $f$ is evaluated at what the inner $f$ outputs, which is $f(1) = 2$:\n$$h'(1) = f'\\big(f(1)\\big)\\cdot f'(1) = f'(2)\\cdot 3 = 4\\cdot 3 = 12$$\n$9 = f'(1)\\cdot f'(1)$ treats both layers as if they were evaluated at $x=1$. $18 = f'(5)\\cdot f'(1)$ uses $f(2)=5$, which is evaluating at the output of $f(f(1))$, one step too far. $24 = f'(2)\\cdot f'(5)$ mixes up both points.","correctAnswer":"a"}}

